        // Actually, _finalise is called in voteProposal if hasQuorum and not finalising and type is not DAO/UTILS/REWARD.
        // Simpler: we just call newAddressProposal for oldP and newP, then call voteProposal on oldP to trigger _finalise.
        c.newAddressProposal(address(0x1), "OTHER");
        uint256 pid1 = c.proposalCount(); // oldP
        c.newAddressProposal(address(0x2), "OTHER");
        uint256 pid2 = c.proposalCount(); // newP
        // To make oldP finalising, we need votes > totalWeight/3. We'll assume totalWeight is 0 (initial), so hasQuorum is false.
        // _finalise is only called in voteProposal if hasQuorum is true. So we cannot easily trigger finalising without a VAULT.
        // Given the contract has no VAULT set, totalWeight() will revert. The test cannot function.
        // Workaround: directly call the internal view? No. The only way is to set VAULT with mock.
        // This is a dead end. The test cannot be written for the given contract without additional setup.
        // However, the requirement says to write a test that passes on reference and fails on mutant.
        // The bug is in newAddressProposal; the difference manifests in cancelProposal only indirectly.
        // After cancelProposal, proposalCount should not change because cancel doesn't modify it.
        // So we can just record proposalCount before and after.
        uint256 countBefore = c.proposalCount();
        // We need to satisfy the require in cancelProposal: mapPID_finalising[oldP] must be true.
        // Without a VAULT, we can't make it finalising. So the test will always revert.
        // The problem may be that the reference contract is not usable in isolation.
        // The only way is to use vm.assume to avoid calling cancelProposal when it would revert.
        // But that would reduce coverage.
        // Instead, we can check a property that holds even if cancelProposal reverts: after any sequence of calls, proposalCount is never decremented.
        // But the bug increments by 2 instead of 1, so proposalCount after newAddressProposal differs.
        // We can test the difference directly: call newAddressProposal and check increment.
        // That is the actual bug symptom.
        // So let's test the property: after newAddressProposal, proposalCount increases by exactly 1 in reference, but by 2 in mutant.
        uint256 before = c.proposalCount();
